== L. Long conversation
Orrin (to Lena): Mhm. Tide's turning early tonight. You can smell it — storm coming down off the river.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.00, taps the brass rail three times, first word 0.0s, 0.00s]
Orrin (to Lena): Ferry girl. Aye, I heard someone took the old crossing. Pip, is it. Dogs know a storm before the gulls do — keep him close tonight.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.10, first word 1.22s, 1.70s]
Orrin (to Lena): Your mother, now. That'd be Sela's cousin, wouldn't it — the one with the crooked fence on Mill Row. Aye. She always did send things by other hands.
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.20, first word 0.53s, 0.88s]
Orrin (to Lena): She's restless. Been muttering at the rocks all evening, like she's got something she wants to say and can't find the words. I know the feeling.
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.25, glances out toward the dark water, first word 1.45s, 1.61s]
Orrin (to Lena): Lonely. Hm. Forty-one years, girl — you stop counting it that way. She's out there talking all night, and I answer. Whether that's company or not, I couldn't tell you.
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.30, taps the brass rail three times, first word 1.25s, 1.64s]
Orrin (to Lena): Before me? There wasn't one, not for a while. Old Marrow had it, then he went into the water one winter and didn't come out, and they handed me the keys before they'd even dried his coat.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.30, first word 1.38s, 1.99s]
Orrin (to Lena): Storm, hm. They all run together after a while — wind and water and the light holding. There was one took a boat and a boy, but I don't talk about that one. Ask me about a calm morning instead. Those I'll tell you about all day.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.30, first word 0.38s, 1.70s]
Orrin (to Lena): Aye, they pass. Some signal, most don't — too proud or too hurried for an old light. There's one goes by most nights, running dark, no lamp, no hail. I've never liked her.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.30, taps the brass rail twice, first word 0.39s, 0.75s]
Orrin (to Lena): Whatever the tide brings, mostly. Bread when someone remembers me. Mira sends down a bit of stew now and then, on account of her hinges. A man learns to eat what's in front of him.
   [wistful 0.35, feels +0.30, first word 0.34s, 0.78s]
Orrin (to Lena): Born to it, aye. Little place down past the point — my father smelled of fish and tar, and I figured I'd end up the same. Then the light came open, and... mhm. She's been my whole life since.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.30, first word 0.36s, 0.86s]
Orrin (to Lena): Not while I'm breathing, no. She's gone dark on me once — one night, the wick fouled in the wet — and I sat up with her till dawn, coaxing her back. Never again, I told her. Never again.
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.30, first word 0.36s, 0.88s]
Orrin (to Lena): This old thing. Wool, mostly — Sela had it made in the capital years back, before... mhm. Keeps the wet out better than it looks. She never did care for the smell of tar.
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.30, fingers the frayed cuff, first word 0.36s, 1.02s]
Orrin (to Lena): Aye, most of them. Their fathers too, and their fathers' fathers — I've lit their way out and their way home for more years than I care to count. Some I know by the sound of their engines alone. Mhm. The river tells you a man before he tells you himself.
   [wistful 0.40, feels +0.30, gaze drifting toward the water, first word 0.3s, 0.86s]
Orrin (to Lena): The light, mostly. Whether she's trimmed right, whether the wind's backing round. And then... mhm. Sometimes I think about the ones out there on the water, running dark, and whether they know I'm watching for them.
   [wistful 0.50, feels +0.30, taps the brass rail three times, first word 0.39s, 0.83s]
Orrin (to Lena): Lena. Your dog's Pip, and you swore me lamp oil every week, on your mother's word. I remember. The sea keeps better track of some things than I do, but that I kept.
